WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.821
Picture a team asked a simple question: which payments were bigger than their own shop's usual payment?

2
00:00:09.821 --> 00:00:14.315
A 5.50 payment is tiny for Volt Electronics, which sells laptops.

3
00:00:14.315 --> 00:00:19.086
At Metro Transit, where a ride costs 2.75, it's twice the usual fare.

4
00:00:19.086 --> 00:00:23.441
So each payment has to be compared with its own shop's average.

5
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.939
The team writes the query the way they'd say it, and tests it on 37 payments.

6
00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:30.257
It answers at once.

7
00:00:30.257 --> 00:00:34.348
On the full data, a million payments, it doesn't come back.

8
00:00:34.348 --> 00:00:37.329
At that pace, it would need about 13 hours.

9
00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:37.607
Why?

10
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.965
Comparing every payment with one overall average is easy.

11
00:00:41.965 --> 00:00:48.609
The subquery, the query in parentheses, runs once, before anything else, and its answer is reused.

12
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.329
This subquery asks for the average of this payment's shop.

13
00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:57.075
That shop number comes from outside, from the row being tested, the outer row.

14
00:00:57.075 --> 00:01:00.361
So the subquery can't run until there is an outer row.

15
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:01.552
A kitchen helps.

16
00:01:01.552 --> 00:01:06.389
The easy subquery is a cook who makes one pot of sauce before dinner starts.

17
00:01:06.389 --> 00:01:10.463
This one is a cook told: for each guest, go and taste the sauce.

18
00:01:10.463 --> 00:01:15.173
One walk to the pot per guest, even when two guests ordered the same dish.

19
00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:18.043
So the guest is the outer row, a payment.

20
00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:20.025
The pot is the payments table.

21
00:01:20.025 --> 00:01:22.735
And each walk is one run of the subquery.

22
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:24.079
Let's watch.

23
00:01:24.079 --> 00:01:26.371
Row 1 belongs to shop 1.

24
00:01:26.371 --> 00:01:30.573
PostgreSQL plugs in 1 and runs the subquery.

25
00:01:30.573 --> 00:01:39.168
It reads all 37 payments, keeps shop 1's seven succeeded ones, and averages them: 4704.86.

26
00:01:39.168 --> 00:01:42.892
4250 isn't bigger, so row 1 is dropped.

27
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:44.615
Row 2, shop 2.

28
00:01:44.615 --> 00:01:52.524
The subquery runs again from the start: another 37 rows, average 1935.

29
00:01:52.524 --> 00:01:54.444
1890 is below it.

30
00:01:54.444 --> 00:01:55.348
Dropped.

31
00:01:55.533 --> 00:02:01.724
Row 3, shop 4: a third run, average 48620.86.

32
00:02:01.724 --> 00:02:07.365
129900 is above it, so payment 3 is kept.

33
00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:09.771
There are 30 succeeded payments.

34
00:02:09.771 --> 00:02:12.429
How many runs, and how many rows read?

35
00:02:12.429 --> 00:02:13.548
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:16.767 --> 00:02:21.492
30 runs, and 1,110 rows read, for only 6 different answers.

37
00:02:21.492 --> 00:02:29.580
Payment 17 also belongs to shop 4, and the subquery works out the same average for it all over again.

38
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:36.533
On the large data set, each run reads all million payments to find one shop's rows.

39
00:02:36.533 --> 00:02:39.400
That's about 51 milliseconds a run.

40
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.595
For the first 20 payments, 1.1 seconds.

41
00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:46.773
For all 930,826 succeeded payments, about 13 hours.

42
00:02:46.773 --> 00:02:50.460
An overnight answer to an afternoon question.

43
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.130
So reading like the question doesn't make a query cheap.

44
00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:58.668
Here PostgreSQL does exactly what the words say, once for every payment.

45
00:02:58.668 --> 00:03:02.512
A subquery that refers to the outer row is called correlated.

46
00:03:02.512 --> 00:03:08.248
EXPLAIN, the command that shows the plan, calls it a SubPlan, and counts its runs as loops.

47
00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:10.385
The fix turns the work around.

48
00:03:10.385 --> 00:03:11.751
Taste each dish once.

49
00:03:11.751 --> 00:03:20.272
One pass over the payments with GROUP BY works out all six shop averages, and keeps them in a hash table, a lookup table in memory.

50
00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:28.088
Then a second pass reads each payment once, and looks up its shop's average in one step.

51
00:03:28.088 --> 00:03:30.687
Two passes, 74 rows read here.

52
00:03:30.687 --> 00:03:39.175
Written as a join, on the large data set, it answers for all 930,826 payments in 282 milliseconds.

53
00:03:39.433 --> 00:03:42.277
Correlated subqueries are not always slow.

54
00:03:42.277 --> 00:03:51.009
PostgreSQL rewrites EXISTS, NOT EXISTS and IN into joins on its own, but not a subquery that works out an average, like this one.

55
00:03:51.233 --> 00:03:58.473
And one that finds its rows through an index, a sorted catalogue, is cheap when the outer query has few rows.

56
00:03:58.473 --> 00:04:05.049
So read the plan: a SubPlan that loops as many times as a big table has rows is the one to rewrite.

57
00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:07.097
One trap: name every column.

58
00:04:07.097 --> 00:04:13.109
Write merchant_id equals merchant_id without the short names, and both find the inner table.

59
00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:18.599
The condition is always true, and you're back to the overall average, with no error.

60
00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:19.991
So, why 13 hours?

61
00:04:19.991 --> 00:04:24.240
A subquery that depends on the outer row runs once per row.

62
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.192
Work it out once for every key, and join.

63
00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:28.839
One quick check.

64
00:04:28.839 --> 00:04:32.065
In the join version, payment 17 comes along.

65
00:04:32.065 --> 00:04:34.777
Is shop 4's average worked out again?

66
00:04:34.777 --> 00:04:35.949
Pause and think.

67
00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:39.507
No.

68
00:04:39.507 --> 00:04:43.133
It's already on its card, 48621.

69
00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:46.419
One look, and one comparison.
